Raw Timing (non-NCU, 15 samples)
========================================
AsT68 median: 0.337280 ms
Accepted baseline: 0.265424 ms
Regression: +27.1% slower (1.271×)

========================================
Root Cause Analysis
========================================
Padding AsT row stride from 64 to 68 caused:
1. Compiler emitted 4 additional registers per thread (40→44)
   - Likely due to non-power-of-2 stride multiplication (k * 68) requiring general multiply
     instead of shift-based addressing for k * 64
2. 44 regs/thread tipped Block Limit Registers from 6→5 blocks/SM
3. Occupancy collapsed: 80.43%→68.10% achieved (5/6 theoretical cap)
4. SMs unable to hide latency with fewer concurrent warps
5. Performance ~27% worse despite intended bank conflict reduction

Bank conflict data: NOT COLLECTED (occupancy collapse makes it irrelevant)
SASS check: NOT PERFORMED (occupancy collapse makes it irrelevant)

========================================
Correctness
========================================
All 9 shapes PASS (256, 5×1024/1000 variants, 768, 512, K1002, N1020)
max_rel=0.005578 max_abs=0.000006 outliers=0 across all shapes

========================================
Conclusion
========================================
REJECTED: Stride padding from 64→68 causes register pressure increase
(40→44) that collapses occupancy from 80.4%→68.1%, making kernel 27% slower.
The register pressure cost dramatically outweighs any potential bank conflict
reduction. This approach is counterproductive for this kernel architecture.

Key learning: Non-power-of-2 shared memory strides on sm_120 can increase
register pressure beyond the occupancy threshold, causing cascading
occupancy and performance collapse. Avoid padding that breaks power-of-2
strides unless register count is verified to stay within the same bracket.

========================================
Next Experiment Proposal: XOR Swizzle (stride stays 64)
========================================
To reduce the 1.63M shared-store bank conflicts without register pressure:
- Use AsT[k][row ^ (k & 0xF)] and Bs[k][col ^ (k & 0xF)] with stride 64
- XOR swizzle breaks power-of-2 bank alignment without changing stride
- Stride remains 64 → no register pressure increase expected
- Both AsT and Bs stores expected to reduce 4-way → 0/1-way conflicts
- Row/col indices stay within [0,63] since (6-bit) ^ (4-bit) ≤ 63
- Test: register_tiled_gemm_Swizzle.cu